    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d407">

                <pb facs="0546" n="436" xml:id="pg436"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2676: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-14T09:00:00+08:00">November 14, 1931—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 14—2:04 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>933. I heard late last night, and had it confirmed this morning, that the
                    Japanese have officially demanded that all Chinese troops be withdrawn 20 li,
                    namely about 6⅔ miles, from Tientsin under provisional note of July 15, 1902
                    (see MacMurray’s <hi rendition="#italic">Treaties</hi>, volume I, page
                        317).<anchor n="12" xml:id="d407-f1"/> Donald states has no knowledge Young
                    Marshal has promised to comply but will increase Tientsin police force.
                        Young<anchor n="13" xml:id="d407-f2"/> claims only about two regiments of
                    Chinese troops were within radius mentioned.</p>
                <p>Repeated Tientsin, Nanking and Commander in Chief.</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Engert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d407-f1" n="12" xml:id="d407-f1-content">
                <p>Note of July 15, 1902, also printed in <ref target="frus1902#pg_198"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1902, p. 198</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d407-f2" n="13" xml:id="d407-f2-content">
                <p>C. Walter Young, an American citizen and writer.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
